import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AutokeyTool from "../components/AutokeyTool.astro";
import AutokeyBreaker from "../components/AutokeyBreaker.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Autokey cipher — encrypt, decrypt, and break online"
  description="Encrypt, decrypt, and break the autokey cipher online, with plaintext or ciphertext keying on a Vigenère or Beaufort tableau. Runs in your browser."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Autokey cipher</h1>
  <p class="mb-8 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    The autokey cipher starts from a short primer keyword and then extends the
    key with the message itself, so the key never repeats. In the plaintext
    variant the key continues with the plaintext; in the ciphertext variant it
    continues with the ciphertext just produced. The tableau sets how each key
    letter combines with the message letter: Vigenère adds it (C = P + K),
    Beaufort subtracts the letter from the key (C = K − P), and variant Beaufort
    subtracts the key from the letter (C = P − K).
  </p>
  <AutokeyTool />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    Break autokey without the primer
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-6 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    Paste ciphertext of at least 26 letters and the solver tries both variants
    on all three tableaux over every primer length, keeping the decryption that
    reads as English. The plaintext variant is recovered exactly; in the
    ciphertext variant the opening letters are reconstructed from context.
  </p>
  <AutokeyBreaker />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    About the autokey cipher
  </h2>
  <div class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    <p>
      This is the cipher Blaise de Vigenère actually invented in 1586. The
      repeating-key cipher that carries his name is Bellaso's; history swapped
      the credit. By feeding the message back into the key, autokey removes the
      periodic repetition that Kasiski's attack and the index of coincidence
      exploit, which made it considerably stronger than the repeating-key cipher
      for three centuries.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not strong by modern standards. The plaintext variant chains errors
      but also chains information: guessing a probable word anywhere in the
      message extends the key around it, and statistical attacks that score
      candidate primers work well with computing power. The ciphertext variant
      is weaker still, since the key beyond the primer is public.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Faq
    items={[
      {
        question:
          "What is the difference between plaintext and ciphertext autokey?",
        answer:
          "Both start the key with a short primer. Plaintext autokey then continues the key with the message itself; ciphertext autokey continues it with the ciphertext already produced. In both, the key never repeats, so the cipher has no period.",
      },
      {
        question: "Who invented the autokey cipher?",
        answer:
          "Blaise de Vigenère described the autokey cipher in 1586. It is stronger than the repeating-key cipher that now carries his name.",
      },
      {
        question: "How is an autokey cipher broken?",
        answer:
          "Ciphertext autokey leaks its key: letters one primer length apart reveal the plaintext once the primer length is guessed. Plaintext autokey has no such shortcut, so the breaker on this page searches for the primer statistically. Both attacks need at least 26 letters, and more is better.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>
